e-fable-5-1"],"explanation":"**What the grid is made of.** Every input is a blank canvas with a single 3×3 block of coloured cells somewhere in it (the fact sheet shows all non-background cells lie in a 3×3 box: rows 6–8 × cols 15–17 in pair 1, rows 2–4 × cols 3–5 in pair 2, rows 1–3 × cols 2–4 in pair 3). The block has a centre cell and 8 surrounding cells.\n\n**What happens.** In the outputs the 3×3 block stays as it is, and every one of its 8 outer cells grows into a ray that runs away from the centre, in the direction that cell sits relative to the centre, all the way to the grid edge, in the cell's own colour:\n\n- edge-neighbours (top, bottom, left, right of the centre) shoot straight rays. In pair 2 the top cell `1` at (2,4) gives a column of 1s up to row 0; the left cell `2` at (3,3) gives a row of 2s to column 0; the bottom `7` runs down column 4 to row 9.\n- corner cells shoot diagonal rays. In pair 2 the `3` at the top-left corner (2,3) gives (1,2), (0,1); the `7` at the bottom-right corner (4,5) gives (5,6), (6,7), (7,8), (8,9).\n- the centre never emits anything (the `5`, `6`, `8` centres stay single cells).\n\nThe fact sheet's \"added segments\" are exactly these 8 rays per pair (some are short because the block is close to an edge, e.g. the 2-cell diagonals in pair 1), so the rule accounts for every changed cell. Rays never collide because they fan out in 8 distinct directions from the centre.\n\n**Test inputs.** Test 1 has its block at rows 2–4 × cols 3–5 with centre 9; test 2 at rows 2–4 × cols 3–5 with centre 7. The same construction applies: locate the bounding box of all non-background cells, take its centre, and for each of the 8 neighbours step in direction (dy, dx) = (neighbour − centre) until the grid ends, painting the neighbour's colour. Grid sizes differ from training (16 and 12) but everything is measured relative to the found block, so that is harmless.\n\nThe program finds the block from the bounding box of the non-background cells, then emits one stage per ray so each edit is a single straight line drawn outward.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    ys, xs = np.where(g != bg)\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = ys.min(), xs.min(), ys.max(), xs.max()\n    cy, cx = (y0 + y1) // 2, (x0 + x1) // 2   # centre of the 3x3 block\n    out = g.copy()\n    for dy in (-1, 0, 1):\n        for dx in (-1, 0, 1):\n            if dy == 0 and dx == 0:\n                continue\n            ny, nx = cy + dy, cx + dx\n            if not (0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W):\n                continue\n            colour = g[ny, nx]\n            if colour == bg:\n                continue\n            y, x = ny + dy, nx + dx\n            drawn = 0\n            while 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W:\n                out[y, x] = colour\n                y += dy\n                x += dx\n                drawn += 1\n            if drawn:\n                emit(out.copy(), f\"extend the colour-{colour} cell at ({ny},{nx}) away from the centre in direction ({dy},{dx}) to the grid edge\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"all eight outer cells of the 3x3 block have been extended into rays; final result\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"ens.** In the outputs the 3×3 block stays as it is, and every one of its 8 outer cells grows into a ray that runs away from the centre, in the direction that cell sits relative to the centre, all the way to the grid edge, in the cell's own colour:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- edge-neighbours (top, bottom, left, right of the centre) shoot straight rays."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- corner cells shoot diagonal rays."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The same construction applies: locate the bounding box of all non-background cells, take its centre, and for each of the 8 neighbours step in direction (dy, dx) = (neighbour − centre) until the grid ends, painting the neighbour's colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The program finds the block from the bounding box of the non-background cells, then emits one stage per ray so each edit is a single straight line drawn outward."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"528334db14c88f3fbd480dfd4512d0e86be488f07e2d8aae7d7d9b8ebd64789b","task_sha256":"62be72f3821ec650a047393d8e7173b7f1e7e4be9430fa27ce6558e2759eb8ad","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
